        ["id": "web-landing", "category": "web", "title": "Landing page", "prompt": "Build a polished landing page for a fictional note-taking app for sailors called Driftwood: index.html with its own CSS and JS files, no frameworks, no external assets. Hero with a call to action, three feature cards, a pricing section with two plans, a footer. Modern and deliberate (typography, spacing, a restrained palette), responsive down to 380px, one tasteful hover or scroll interaction. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "web-app", "category": "web", "title": "Pomodoro timer", "prompt": "Write a Pomodoro timer as a small static web app (index.html plus CSS and JS files, no libraries): 25/5 minute cycles with a big countdown, start/pause/reset, a session counter, a settings row to change both durations, keyboard shortcuts (space to start/pause, r to reset), and state that survives a reload via localStorage. Clean, keyboard-accessible. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "web-dashboard", "category": "web", "title": "Data dashboard", "prompt": "Generate a CSV of 365 days of plausible daily sales for four product lines (write it as data.csv), then build a static dashboard (index.html, no chart libraries, hand-drawn SVG or canvas) that loads it and shows a monthly revenue line per product, a bar chart of the top weekdays, and three headline numbers. It must work when opened as a plain file, so inline the data or load it in a way that needs no server. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "game-snake", "category": "game", "title": "Snake with polish", "prompt": "Build Snake as a single index.html with inline CSS and JS: canvas rendering, arrow and WASD keys, a score, increasing speed, a proper game-over screen with restart, a persistent high score, and one visual flourish of your choice. It must play well at 60 fps in a desktop browser. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "cli-todo", "category": "cli", "title": "Command-line todo tool", "prompt": "Write a command-line todo manager in Python 3 (standard library only) as todo.py: add, list, done, remove, and a --json flag on list; state stored in todo.json next to the script; helpful errors; a README.md with examples. Include a tests.py that exercises every command through subprocess and passes. Run the tests yourself. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present the README and the test output.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "cli-log-stats", "category": "cli", "title": "Log analyser", "prompt": "Generate a realistic 5,000-line web server access log (write it as access.log, Apache combined format, with a handful of anomalies: a burst from one IP, a spike of 500s, a very slow endpoint). Then write analyse.py (Python 3, standard library) that prints a report: top paths, top IPs, status distribution, and the anomalies it found, with a one-line explanation of how each was detected. Run it and save the output to report.txt. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present report.txt.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "code-parser", "category": "coding", "title": "ISO 8601 duration parser", "prompt": "Write a Python module durations.py with parse_duration(s: str) -> int that parses ISO 8601 durations like \"PT1H30M\", \"P2DT3H4M5S\" and \"P1W\" into total seconds, raising ValueError on invalid input, plus a test_durations.py with at least ten cases covering the edge cases you can think of. Run the tests with python3 -m unittest and make them pass. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present the module.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "code-refactor", "category": "coding", "title": "Fix and test a buggy function", "prompt": "Create users.js containing exactly this code, then fix every bug in it without changing the public function names, and write a test file (plain Node, node:test) that fails on the original and passes on your fix. Run the tests. Explain each bug in one line in NOTES.md.\n\nasync function loadUsers(ids) {\n  const out = {};\n  ids.forEach(async (id) => {\n    const r = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n    out[id] = r.json();\n  });\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(list) {\n  return list.filter((x, i) => list.indexOf(x) == i || x == null);\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { loadUsers, dedupe };\n\nWhen you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present NOTES.md.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "data-sql", "category": "data", "title": "SQLite report", "prompt": "Using Python 3 and its built-in sqlite3, create shop.db with tables customers, orders and order_items filled with a few hundred rows of plausible generated data, then write report.py that answers: revenue per month, the ten best customers by lifetime value, and the product most often bought together with the best-selling product. Print the answers as aligned text tables and save them to report.md. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present report.md.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "write-brief", "category": "writing", "title": "Technical design brief", "prompt": "Write DESIGN.md: a design brief for adding recurring-transaction detection (same payee, similar amount, roughly monthly) to a Node.js CLI that reads a CSV of transactions. Data structures, the detection heuristic with concrete thresholds, edge cases, the tests you would write, and what you would deliberately leave out of a first version. Under 900 words, no filler. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present DESIGN.md.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "write-explain", "category": "writing", "title": "Explain it to a ten-year-old", "prompt": "Write EXPLAIN.md explaining, in at most 300 words, how a computer stores a picture, for a curious ten-year-old. One concrete example, no unexplained jargon, and a final paragraph that anticipates the obvious follow-up question. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present EXPLAIN.md.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "reason-estimate", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Fermi estimate with a model", "prompt": "Estimate how many piano tuners work in Berlin. Write estimate.py that encodes every assumption as a named variable with a plausible range, runs a simple Monte Carlo over those ranges, and prints the median and an 80% interval. Then write ANSWER.md with the assumptions, the result, and which assumption the answer is most sensitive to. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present ANSWER.md.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "reason-puzzle", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Constraint puzzle, solved and verified", "prompt": "Five houses in a row, each a different colour, owner nationality, pet, drink and hobby, with the fifteen classic Einstein-puzzle clues (write the clue set you use into PUZZLE.md). Solve it by writing a small constraint solver (any language available here), print the full solution, and have the program verify every clue against it. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present the program's output.", "builtin": true],
        ["id": "agent-repo", "category": "agentic", "title": "Small library with docs and tests", "prompt": "Create a tiny but complete library for parsing and formatting semantic versions (semver) in the language you consider best for it: parse, compare, satisfy a range like \"^1.2.0\" or \">=2 <3\", and bump. Package layout, a README.md with examples, and a test suite you run and make pass. When you are done, call the arena present tool with what the judge should look at (a file, a URL, or text). Work on your own: there is no user to ask, so decide every open question yourself. Present the README.", "builtin": true],
